\f1\fs22 The survival instinct that had carried her through the PAPER attack was still firing. Hayley couldn\'92t go home\'97not to Chainsaw\'92s needy meowing and furniture demolition, not to an apartment they might be watching. Instead, she\'92d found refuge in a 24-hour internet caf\'e9 in the Pale, claiming a table in the back corner where she could watch both exits. Her near-death experience had crystallised one thing: Anthony Chantara was at the centre of something much larger than a simple bombing case.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Give me everything on ENCHANTED Cleaning,\'94 Hayley quietly instructed the puter and prepared herself for disappointment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley was not disappointed. Windows opened; tables of data, and galleries of images appeared, but as Hayley examined the results, nothing focussed directly on Chantara\'92s cleaning business. Rather, the results popped out tangentially and associatively from such things as business reviews sites (ENCHANTED Cleaning\'92s ratings were evenly spread between one mop and five mops), small business awards (ENCHANTED Cleaning earned the Filthy Bucket Award five years running from the Tasman Cleaners\'92 Alliance) and accounts payment systems. Hayley regarded the business awards as probably the most reliable of those three, but plunged into larger corpus of accounts payable records anyway.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Consolidate a list of customers of ENCHANTED Cleaning customers.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There were thousands. Hayley rolled her eyes. \'92Sort by total payment value, year to date.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The sorted list confirmed Hayley\'92s suspicions. Apparently, ENCHANTED Cleaning could count among its best customers almost all of the leading corporations in Ulyssia, all of whom were generating phoney invoices, all playing Ulyssia\'92s unofficial national sport: tax avoidance. If all those payments weren\'92t fake, Anthony Chantara would be a billionaire. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'92Typical,\'92 Hayley said to herself, but the computer took her words as gospel. It said, 
\f2\i analysing
\f1\i0 , and threw a spinner up on its screen.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'92No! I didn\'92t\'97\'92 Hayley attacked keys with her middle fingers and, when that didn\'92t work, she bashed the whole keyboard with first one fist, then both. Exasperated, she pushed back from the puter. At the caf\'e9 counter, she ordered coffee, but changed her mind and asked for a cola instead\'97full sugar, she needed the caffeine hit. She cracked the ring-pull and guzzled on the way back to her table.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On the puter was a new table of debtor names with a new column alongside called 
\fs18 DESCRIPTION
\fs22 . Hayley scrolled; the column was entirely blank. \'92Eliminate rows with empty descriptions.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The table folded and blurred, then presented new rows of data with text in the description column, things like: 
\f2\i Deep Clean: Eviction
\f1\i0 , or 
\f2\i Deep Clean: Bar Fight
\f1\i0 , or 
\f2\i Deep Clean: Bachelor Party,
\f1\i0  or 
\f2\i Deep Clean: Cock Fight
\f1\i0 . Then Hayley saw a row that caught her attention.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Deep Clean: Crime Scene\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And the 
\fs18 CUSTOMER NAME
\fs22  column read, 
\f2\i Department of Traffic Movement and Property Security.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s jaw dropped in the manner of a sack of concrete falling off the back of a truck. \'92The police?\'92 she shouted into the next room, then scrolled fast and frantically through the table, searching. There were more of the same description scattered though the table. Then Hayley found a whole clump of them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'92Filter for description 
\f2\i Deep Clean
\f1\i0 : 
\f2\i Crime Scene
\f1\i0  and sort by most recent.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The screened presented a table of accounts paid records. Dozens of them. Hayley double-tapped the first; its details swelled into a new window. The record had a case number assigned, so Hayley signed into the Police Department system and looked it up. A window popped up with the text, 
\fs18 CASE CLOSED: SUICIDE
\fs20 .\

\fs22 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The window was meant as warning against investigators meddling into matters which could get them maimed or killed, but Hayley ordered, \'92Open file,\'92 anyway, and submitted to the seven-factor interrogation to gain access.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What Hayley read could get her maimed 
\f2\i and
\f1\i0  killed. The lead investigator was Alexander Shakti, so now she not only had PAPER on her arse, but probably Shakti Enterprises as well. She put her palms flat on the table either side of her puter, stared down at her knees and drew a long, slow breath. 
\f2\i Now what?
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley browsed the details of the file, such as they were. In typical Shakti fashion, the case had been shut down quickly and there was no record of evidence at all, other than a few photos of the victim: Travis Teh, shotgun wound under the jaw, upper skull blown to gooey popcorn. Hayley checked the date: early March. Wait, was that the same day the bomb exploded? Hayley opened that case file. Yes it was. She flipped through the various attachments and saw Shakti led a Tactical Response team to quell a riot after the explosion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley loved coincidences, because they mostly weren\'92t.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chantara and Shakti were both at the bomb site. Sarah Jacobson was there.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chantara and Shakti both attended the Teh suicide.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The apparent suicide and the bombing occurred, respectively, in the wee hours of the morning and the early evening of the same day. Hayley was nothing if not thorough; she wanted to know Jacobson\'92s whereabouts and activities for that day. Sarah Jacobson, being Airborne, was both easier and harder to track than the usual Cloudborne targets of police fit-ups. There was no day-to-day transactional data kept, largely because only Cloudborne were fitted identifiers in their armpits. If Sarah bought fast food at a particular location on a particular day, Hayley would learn none of that. But if she attended a Gala Charity event, or was seen wearing a certain kind of dress, or slept with someone salacious, or was admitted to hospital with life-threatening injuries\'85\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley discovered Sarah was admitted to hospital with life-threatening injuries, on the night of the bombing, though there seemed to be no record of an ambulance taking her there. It was the most recent record of Sarah\'92s existence. Hayley double-checked Sarah\'92s discharge date\'97only two days after her admission, and inconsistent with suffering the grave description of her state when she arrived. Even curiouser, Jacobson discharged herself, and a picture of her taken at the time appeared to show her in perfect health. Hayley scrolled down to the bottom of the window and found a mostly blank space in which were some imaged-captured words in a doctor\'92s chicken-scratch. Hayley squinted hard at the words until she made them resolve into, 
\f2\i Came back and stole a wheelchair
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Previous to the bombing and the hospital, Hayley saw Jacobson had attended a routine medical check-up for a new job\'97quite a significant promotion. According to her career chart, Sarah was moving up the Cyber Security career ladder like she was in a chair lift. The day of the medical exam was Jacobson\'92s first day at her new place of employment; the name seemed familiar. Hayley cycled through the windows stacked on her tablet screen, and there it was, in the Travis Teh file: the name of Sarah Jacobson\'92s employer. Her new place of work was where Teh worked until his grisly end in the dark hours of the morning of Jacobson\'92s first day, which brought Shakti and then Chantara to their offices, on the same day on which Jacobson and Chantara were later thought to be a couple by Chantara\'92s dubious friends, minutes before they were injured in a bomb blast.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Take that\'97tax you!\'97coincidence.
